<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="52001" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN/529/52001.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="CMN" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="529" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN/529.xml">Seminar Communication Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>SC </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Political Communication</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This seminar provides an overview of research on contemporary political communication, primarily in the social sciences tradition. Students are introduced to the pressing theories and concepts that have motivated the field in recent years. The course mainly focuses on mediated political communication, with a strong emphasis on questions about political ontology, identity, what drives political media use, political information processing, and democratic consequences.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="S4 ">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4103</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Coles" firstName="S">Coles, S</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>